  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Alice and Bob have an array of AAA of NNN elements, and will play a game on it. They alternate turns, with Alice going first.\n\nOn each turn:\n\n\nLet MMM denote the current length of AAA.\n\nIf M=1M = 1M=1, the game stops. Otherwise, the current player must make a move.\nOn Alice's turn, she must choose integers LLL and RRR (1≤L&lt;R≤M1 \\leq L \\lt R \\leq M1≤L&lt;R≤M), delete the elements AL,AL+1,…,ARA_L, A_{L+1}, \\ldots, A_RAL​,AL+1​,…,AR​ from the array,\n\nand insert ∣AL+AL+1+…+AR∣|A_L + A_{L+1} + \\ldots + A_R|∣AL​+AL+1​+…+AR​∣ in their place.\n\nNote that ∣x∣|x|∣x∣ denotes the absolute value of xxx.\nOn Bob's turn, he must choose integers LLL and RRR (1≤L&lt;R≤M1 \\leq L \\lt R \\leq M1≤L&lt;R≤M), delete the elements AL,AL+1,…,ARA_L, A_{L+1}, \\ldots, A_RAL​,AL+1​,…,AR​ from the array, and insert (AL+AL+1+…+AR)(A_L + A_{L+1} + \\ldots + A_R)(AL​+AL+1​+…+AR​) in their place.\nThat is, Alice must choose some subarray, delete it, and insert the absolute value of its sum in its place.\n\nBob will do the same, but insert just the sum (and not its absolute value).\n\nNote that since L&lt;RL \\lt RL&lt;R, the length of the array will decrease after each move.\n\nAlice wants to maximize the value of the final element, while Bob wants to minimize it.\n\nIf both Alice and Bob make their moves optimally, what will the value of the final element be?\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains one integer NNN — the number of elements in AAA.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​, denoting the array AAA.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the value of the final element if both players move optimally.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤1051 \\leq N \\leq 10^51≤N≤105",
      "−109≤Ai≤109-10^9 \\leq A_i\\leq 10^9−109≤Ai​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 4⋅1054 \\cdot 10^54⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n1\n1\n2\n5 -7\n6\n1 1 -2 5 -2 -1",
        "output": "1\n2\n8",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The array is already of length 111, so no moves will be made.\nTest case 222: Since L<RL \\lt RL<R, Alice has no choice but to choose the entire array [5,−7][5, -7][5,−7] and replace it with ∣5+(−7)∣=2|5 + (-7)| = 2∣5+(−7)∣=2, at which point the game ends.\nTest case 333: One possible sequence of moves is as follows:\n\nAlice chooses L=5L = 5L=5 and R=6R = 6R=6, replacing the subarray [−2,−1][-2, -1][−2,−1] with its absolute sum 333.\nThe array is now A=[1,1,−2,5,3]A = [1, 1, -2, 5, 3]A=[1,1,−2,5,3].\nBob chooses L=2L = 2L=2 and R=4R = 4R=4, replacing the subarray [1,−2,5][1, -2, 5][1,−2,5] with its sum 444.\nThe array is now A=[1,4,3]A = [1, 4, 3]A=[1,4,3].\nAlice chooses L=2L = 2L=2 and R=3R = 3R=3, turning the array into A=[1,7]A = [1, 7]A=[1,7].\nBob chooses L=1L = 1L=1 and R=2R = 2R=2, turning the array into A=[8]A = [8]A=[8]."
      }
    ]
  },
